                <pb facs="1111" n="995" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg995"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 312.115/156.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        Consul General Hanna</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-03-23">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 23, 1915</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your telegram March 15. It is proper to protest in behalf of Americans against a
                    tax which is confiscatory or discriminatory, and you are also instructed to use
                    your good offices for the protection of other foreigners against such taxation,
                    but we are not justified in inquiring into its purpose or the use to be made of
                    it if levied by a competent de facto authority. You will constantly bear in mind
                    the fact that injustice done to religious bodies will arouse deep and lasting
                    prejudice against those responsible for it and therefore use such moral
                    influence as is possible against such acts.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bryan.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
